{
  // One row per student who has told us to stop emailing them. Every nudge
  // email ends with "To stop these emails, turn them off in your settings", so
  // this entity is the thing that makes that sentence true.
  //
  // It is its own entity rather than a field on User or StudentProfile on
  // purpose. User.jsonc already disagrees with the live rows (it declares
  // `onboarding_complete` while every line of code reads `onboarding_completed`,
  // and live rows carry fields the schema does not declare), so a sync could
  // strip real data off accounts. StudentProfile is wrong too: 10 of 86
  // accounts have no profile row at all, and an opt out that cannot be recorded
  // is an opt out we did not honour.
  //
  // Opting back in soft deletes the row rather than removing it. This repo soft
  // deletes everywhere, and "they turned it off in March and back on in April"
  // is worth keeping.
  "name": "NudgeOptOut",
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "user_id": {
      "type": "string"
    },
    "opted_out_at": {
      "type": "string",
      "format": "date-time"
    },
    "reason": {
      "type": "string"
    },
    "source": {
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
        "settings",
        "answer_page",
        "email_link",
        "admin"
      ],
      "default": "settings"
    },
    "deletion_status": {
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
        "active",
        "deleted",
        "permanently_deleted"
      ],
      "default": "active"
    },
    "deleted_at": {
      "type": "string",
      "format": "date-time"
    },
    "purge_at": {
      "type": "string",
      "format": "date-time"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "user_id"
  ],
  // A property is addressed as `data.<field>`. A bare `"user_id"` matches
  // nothing, fails silently, and looks exactly like a student who never opted
  // out. `created_by_id` is a system field and is the one key that stays bare.
  // StudentNudge, NetworkNotification and BlockedUser are the live examples of
  // both forms.
  //
  // `create` is keyed on `created_by_id` rather than on `data.user_id` because
  // this is the one rule a student has to be able to satisfy while the row does
  // not exist yet, and creator scoping is the form this app is known to enforce
  // (PilotEvent, ContinuationInterest and NetworkNotification all create this
  // way). Admin is in the list so the scheduled job and a support request can
  // both record one.
  //
  // That leaves one hole worth naming, and naming accurately: **the create rule
  // below does not stop a student writing a row that carries somebody else's
  // `user_id`.** The server fills `created_by_id` in for whoever is signed in,
  // so the rule is satisfied for every signed in student no matter what is in
  // `data`. Victim ids are not secret either: NetworkProfile, Follow and User
  // all declare `read: null`. Left there, any student can silence our emails to
  // any other student.
  //
  // What actually closes it is `isOptedOut` in
  // base44/shared/nudge-response.js, which counts a row only when
  // `created_by_id` equals `user_id`. There is no exemption. `source` is a
  // field on the row a student writes, so `source: "admin"` is the attack and
  // not the exception, and a row with no `created_by_id` at all does not count
  // either. Do not reintroduce either exemption on the grounds that the rules
  // below look tight, and do not add a field level check here: Base44 has no
  // field level RLS, which is the whole reason the check lives in code.
  //
  // `admin` stays in the `source` enum because it is a label, not a
  // permission. Nothing today writes it. If an admin ever needs to record an
  // opt out on a student's behalf, resolve `created_by_id` against the loaded
  // `User` rows and require `role === "admin"` there, where the answer comes
  // from a table a student cannot write.
  //
  // `read` and `update` key off `user_id` as well as `created_by_id` so a
  // student can always read and turn off whatever names them. `delete` stays
  // admin only: opting back in is a soft delete, which is an update.
  //
  // State: proposed. No NudgeOptOut row exists yet, so these rules have never
  // been exercised by a real student account against the live API. They match
  // the shape of rules that are live on other entities. That is not the same as
  // verified, so read one back as a real student before saying it is.
  "rls": {
    "create": {
      "$or": [
        {
          "created_by_id": "{{user.id}}"
        },
        {
          "user_condition": {
            "role": "admin"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    "read": {
      "$or": [
        {
          "data.user_id": "{{user.id}}"
        },
        {
          "created_by_id": "{{user.id}}"
        },
        {
          "user_condition": {
            "role": "admin"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    "update": {
      "$or": [
        {
          "data.user_id": "{{user.id}}"
        },
        {
          "created_by_id": "{{user.id}}"
        },
        {
          "user_condition": {
            "role": "admin"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    "delete": {
      "user_condition": {
        "role": "admin"
      }
    }
  }
}
